---
onetaskgraph_template: 1
description: >-
  The design document a plan is read and approved as. The variables below are its data; how
  to write each answer is stated in this template's guidance comments, which no rendering
  carries.
variables:
  what:
    description: >-
      The What section's prose.
    type: text
    required: true
  why:
    description: >-
      The Why section's prose: why the work is worth doing, as its impact on the product,
      its users, development and resources, never an account of who asked for it.
    type: text
    required: true
  goals:
    description: >-
      The Goals section's entries, one string per goal, or `[]` for a plan whose tasks state
      none.
    type: list
    items: string
    required: false
    default: []
  architecture:
    description: >-
      The Architecture section's overview only, which may show a diagram as an image
      reference `![<alt text>](./<name>.png)` to an asset of this document. Each unit's
      subsection is rendered from `units`.
    type: text
    required: true
  units:
    description: >-
      One object per unit that changes, in reading order, with these keys: `name` (string,
      the unit's label, which a planned task's `unit` names); `repository` (string, the
      repository's short name, such as `onepipeline`); `part` (string, the package or path
      within that repository, or `""` when the unit is the whole repository); `summary`
      (text, the unit's one paragraph, which may show a diagram as an image reference
      `![<alt text>](./<name>.png)` to an asset of this document); `reversible` (a list of objects, the unit's trivial and
      low-cost decisions in reading order, or `[]` when it has none), each with the string
      keys `title` (its one-line summary) and `text` (its one sentence); and `decisions` (a
      list of objects, the unit's high-cost decisions, or `[]` when it has none). Each decision has the keys `name`
      (string, its heading), `justification` (string, naming who would have to act to undo
      it), `content` (a nonempty ordered list of parts) and, only when it has examples,
      `examples` (a nonempty ordered list of parts of the same shape, omitted otherwise).
      Each part is exactly either
      {type: "prose", text: string} or {type: "code", language: string, text: string}.
      Prose is nonblank Markdown explanation without fenced-code blocks; inline code and
      an image reference `![<alt text>](./<name>.png)` to an asset of this document are
      allowed. Code is nonblank literal text, never a pre-fenced block. Language is a
      nonempty single-line fence info token: letters, digits, underscore, plus or hyphen;
      use `text` for untyped output. No extra keys beyond `examples`, no mixed shapes and
      no unknown tags. Exact legacy decisions instead retain `name`, `justification`,
      `summary` and `artifact`, all strings, rendered historically without classification,
      and take no `examples`. Decisions
      may use either exact shape while deliberately regenerating a document.
    type: list
    items: object
    required: true
  acceptance_criteria:
    description: >-
      One criterion per entry, each a string.
    type: list
    items: string
    required: true
  graph:
    description: >-
      The plan's tasks and their dependencies exactly as `onetaskgraph project graph
      <project> --format json --group-by orchestrator.unit` prints them: the Planned tasks
      table lists every node, its unit (`group`) and the titles it depends on, from this.
    type: object
    required: false
    default: {}
  delivers:
    description: >-
      What each task delivers, as a short phrase on one line, keyed by the task's qualified
      id as `graph` names it.
    type: object
    required: false
    default: {}
  planned_tasks:
    description: >-
      Kept for documents written before `graph`: one object per task with five string keys,
      `task`, `unit`, `delivers`, `depends_on` and `location`. A new document answers `graph`
      and `delivers` instead and leaves this `[]`.
    type: list
    items: object
    required: false
    default: []
  dag_image:
    description: >-
      The bare file name of the image asset of this document that draws the plan's tasks
      and their dependencies, each inside the unit it changes, as its content references it
      with `./<name>`; `""` for a plan that shows no such image.
    type: text
    required: false
    default: ""
  plan_budgets:
    description: >-
      The plan's plan-level budget answers, its project's `orchestrator.plan-budgets`
      metadata as the plan store holds it, or `{}` for a plan with no project analysis: the
      shape the `plan-description` template's `plan_budgets` schema declares.
    type: object
    required: false
    default: {}
  budgets:
    description: >-
      Every budget of every task of the plan, read from the tasks' `orchestrator.budgets`
      metadata, or `[]`: each object is the budget as its task's `orchestrator.budgets`
      record holds it, the shape the `plan-task-budgets` template's schema declares, with
      four keys added, `node` (the id of the task
      that owns it), `location` (where the plan store reports that task), `anchor` (the
      anchor of the budget's heading in that task, or `""` for a legacy budget) and `link`
      (the destination of the budget's reference link: a local task's file relative to this
      document's directory, any other location as it stands, then `#` and the anchor).
    type: list
    items: object
    required: false
    default: []
  visual_changes:
    description: >-
      One object per visual change the plan expects, in the order the plan's
      visual spike's report lists them, with required non-empty string keys: `name` (the change's short
      heading), `description` (what is changing, in plain words), `after` and optional `before` (each
      the bare file name of an image asset of this document, as its content references it
      with `./<name>`). Only absence of `before` selects a single image; null, empty
      and non-string values are invalid. `after` is always required. `[]` for a plan with no visual change.
    type: list
    items: object
    required: false
    default: []
  accepted_opaque:
    description: >-
      Copy verbatim from the project's `onepipeline.export_edges` metadata: ordered export edges,
      each with `source`, `to` and `accepted_opaque` (an ordered list of opaque pattern strings).
    type: list
    items: object
    required: false
    default: []
---
{#- A table cell is one line holding no bare column delimiter, whatever an answer says. -#}
{% macro cell(value) %}{{ value | replace("|", "\\|") | replace("\r\n", " ") | replace("\n", " ") | replace("\r", " ") }}{% endmacro %}
{#- An expandable block's summary is HTML: `&`, `<` and `>` as entities, each backtick span as `<code>`. -#}
{% macro summary(title) %}{% for piece in title | replace("&", "&amp;") | replace("<", "&lt;") | replace(">", "&gt;") | split("`") %}{% if loop.index0 is odd and not loop.last %}<code>{{ piece }}</code>{% elif loop.index0 is odd %}`{{ piece }}{% else %}{{ piece }}{% endif %}{% endfor %}{% endmacro %}
{#- One prose or code part of a decision, a code part fenced longer than any backtick run it holds. -#}
{% macro part(one) %}
{% if one.type == "prose" %}
{{ one.text }}

{% else %}
{% set fence = namespace(value="```") %}
{% for unused in range((one.text | length) - (one.text | replace("`", "") | length)) %}
{% set fence.value = fence.value + "`" %}
{% endfor %}
{{ fence.value }}{{ one.language }}
{{ one.text }}
{{ fence.value }}

{% endif %}
{% endmacro %}
{% block document_guidance %}
{#
  THE READER. A technical product manager with no depth in this domain. They read this
  document instead of the plan, and must be able to accept a hard-to-reverse decision, argue
  with the architecture, or say the acceptance criteria do not describe what was asked.

  PROSE. Plain, simple, terse and precise, and no more technical than the plan's own goal
  statement. A term that cannot be avoided is defined once, in passing.

  LENGTH. A document is about as long as the design document this template replaces would
  be for the same plan. Exactness is spent on what is hard to undo, and everything else is
  said in a sentence. A high-cost decision's content is about 1,200 characters or less,
  examples excluded. The whole document's composed issue body, as `onetaskgraph document
  copy --dry-run` sizes it, is under 45,000 characters, reached
  through terser writing and never by dropping what the approver needs. A plan that cannot
  be documented under 45,000 characters without losing needed content stays under GitHub's
  65,536-character refusal, and its writer reports the measured figure rather than
  truncating.

  SECTIONS. In this order: What, Why, Goals, Visual changes, Architecture, Budgets,
  Acceptance criteria and Planned tasks. Goals appears only for a plan whose tasks state
  goals, and Visual changes only for a plan with a visual change. A
  Budgets visibility follows `budgets_guidance` below. Planned tasks opens with an image of the plan's tasks and their dependencies only for a plan of
  more than one task with at least one dependency edge, and that image is generated by the
  flow from the plan, never drawn by the writer.
  There is no separate Contracts section, and no answer adds a heading of its own.

  IMAGES. A picture is an image asset of this document, never a Mermaid code block, which a
  Linear board does not render. Architecture is where a diagram is most often worth it, and
  the ARCHITECTURE guidance says when and how one is drawn, shown and stored; any other
  section may show one the same way where a picture explains better than prose.

  HIGHLIGHTING. No GitHub alert blocks (`> [!CAUTION]` and the like) anywhere. The bold
  reversibility line is the whole of the highlighting.

  EXPANDABLE BLOCKS. What is only sometimes needed — a reversible decision's sentence, a
  decision's examples, the planned-tasks table — is collapsed, and the template alone writes
  each block, in the authored form onetaskgraph's README states under "Expandable blocks".
  No answer writes a `<details>` block of its own.

  THIS TEMPLATE. Each section renders inside its own block: `what`, `why`, `goals`,
  `visual_changes`, `architecture`, `budgets`, `acceptance_criteria` and `planned_tasks`. Each section's guidance is a Jinja comment in a
  separate block, `<name>_guidance`, and this block, `document_guidance`, holds what applies
  to the whole document. Guidance is always a Jinja comment, never an HTML comment, so a
  rendered document carries none of it. The front matter states the data shape, what each
  answer and key holds; the judgment lives here. A repository overrides any one block, a
  section's guidance without its rendering or the other way round, through the engine's
  repository layer: `.onepipeline/templates/design-doc.md.j2` in its checkout, a file
  beginning `{% extends "onepipeline/host/design-doc.md.j2" %}` that redefines only the
  blocks it names.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block what_guidance %}
{#
  WHAT. What is being built. What and Why are each a few plain sentences in plain terms a
  non-specialist reads, and carry no interface detail and no implementation detail.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block what %}
## What

{{ what }}

{% endblock %}
{% block why_guidance %}
{#
  WHY. Why the work is worth doing, from the product owner's point of view: its impact on
  the product, its users, development and resources. It never narrates who asked for what:
  no "the user asked", "the user said" or "the brief says", and no quoted request. The
  record of what was requested and what was decided has one home, the plan's own
  description, and is not in this document; a task's own Why states impact too, never that
  record. Speaking of the product's users as the people the work affects is impact, not
  attribution.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block why %}
## Why

{{ why }}

{% endblock %}
{% block goals_guidance %}
{#
  GOALS. The plan's tasks' Goals merged for the reader: one bullet per outcome the plan must
  reach, written from what the tasks' own `## Goals` sections state and inventing none. Where
  several tasks state one outcome, it appears once. A non-functional goal keeps the budget it
  names, by that budget's `id`, as its task states it. What a goal is is stated once, in the
  `goals` variable of this host's `plan-task` template. A plan whose tasks state no goals
  answers `[]` and this document has no Goals section.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block goals %}
{% if goals %}
## Goals

{% for goal in goals %}
- {{ goal | replace("\n", "\n  ") }}
{% endfor %}

{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
{% block visual_changes_guidance %}
{#
  VISUAL CHANGES. One `### <name>` subsection per change the plan expects to make to what a
  user sees: a web page or component, a TUI, or a CLI's printed output. Each says in the
  user's terms what changes, then shows the required after image and optional before image.
  The images are illustrative captures from a throwaway, mocked spike, not a preview of the
  finished work, which may differ where production concerns require.

  The `visual_changes` answer restates the plan's visual spike report, answer for answer,
  and each image it names is an asset of this document; the writer invents none. A plan
  with no visual change answers `[]` and this document has no Visual changes section.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block visual_changes %}
{% if visual_changes %}
## Visual changes

{% for change in visual_changes %}
### {{ change.name }}

{{ change.description }}

{% if change.before is defined %}
**Before**

![Before: {{ change.name }}](./{{ change.before }})

**After**

![After: {{ change.name }}](./{{ change.after }})
{% else %}
![{{ change.name }}](./{{ change.after }})
{% endif %}

{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
{% block architecture_guidance %}
{#
  ARCHITECTURE. The `architecture` answer is a short overview, two to four sentences saying
  how the units fit. A unit is a repository, or a part of a project. Every unit that changes
  gets one subsection, headed `### <unit> — `<repository>` (`<package or path>`)`; the
  parenthesis appears only when the unit is part of a repository.

  Under each unit heading:
    - One paragraph. It says in plain words what the unit is and what changes in it. It
      does not list the unit's individual decisions; those follow.
    - One `#### <decision>` per high-cost decision the unit owns, and only for those, so a
      unit with none has no `####`. Each opens with `**Reversibility: high cost** —` and a
      justification naming who would have to act to undo it. Then one or two plain
      sentences say what the decision means. Supply ordered prose/code parts, interleaving
      explanations and literal artifacts as needed. The template owns every language label
      and safe backtick fence. Never author fences, and never disguise explanation as code.
      A prose-only behavioural decision is valid. For literal artifacts use: a `diff` block for
      a changed shape, the full shape for a new one, a `--help` diff for a CLI, a text
      sketch for a UI. A behavioural decision with no artifact says so in one sentence.
    - Optionally, `examples` after a decision's content, rendered collapsed under the
      summary Examples. Examples are given only where the abstract contract is hard to
      follow without them: a concrete, illustrative call, record, input and output, or
      before and after. Keep them to about 800 characters per decision.
    - Then, after the high-cost subsections, a `**Reversible:**` line followed by one
      expandable block per trivial or low-cost decision the unit makes: an interface, a
      stored shape, a flag, a behaviour. Its `title` is the block's summary and its `text`
      the body. A title says what the decision changes in that area, not only the area:
      "Adds a `--dry-run` flag to `copy`", not "Flag name". It fits on one line of about 90
      characters and ends with no period; the template writes its backtick code as the
      summary's HTML. The text stays one sentence naming the thing exactly (the flag, key,
      field, route or command). An item carries no level label, no artifact and no
      justification, because trivial and low cost read the same here. A unit with no such
      decision has no `**Reversible:**` line.
    - Nothing else: no Kind, Owner or Status line, no Owes or May-assume bullets, and no
      links between sections.

  DIAGRAMS. A diagram earns its place when the architecture has three or more units, or a
  flow or a boundary between units, whose relations prose would need more than a short
  paragraph to convey. A single unit, or anything one sentence says, gets no diagram, and a
  document holds a few diagrams at most. Write it as a Mermaid source, render it with `just
  render-diagram <source.mmd> <name>.png`, show it from the `architecture` overview, a
  unit's `summary`, or the prose part of a decision as `![<alt text saying what it
  shows>](./<name>.png)`, and give the file to the store with `--asset` when the document is
  stored.

  REVERSIBILITY. Three levels, defined by who would have to act to undo the decision:
    - trivial: only the unit that owns it, in one ordinary change.
    - low cost: parties we control, changing together. For example, several of our
      repositories or tools updated in step, a deprecation window, or a migration of data
      we own.
    - high cost: parties we don't control or can't list, or data we can't rewrite. For
      example, a public flag or field other people script against, a released artifact, or
      stored data that older installed versions or outside readers keep reading.
  When in doubt, the stricter level. These definitions are guidance a repository may
  override by replacing this block.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block architecture %}
## Architecture

{{ architecture }}

{% for unit in units %}
### {{ unit.name }} — `{{ unit.repository }}`{% if unit.part %} (`{{ unit.part }}`){% endif %}


{{ unit.summary }}

{% for decision in unit.decisions %}
#### {{ decision.name }}

**Reversibility: high cost** — {{ decision.justification }}

{% if decision.content is defined %}
{% for one in decision.content %}
{{ part(one) -}}
{% endfor %}
{% if decision.examples is defined %}
<details>
<summary>Examples</summary>

{% for one in decision.examples %}
{{ part(one) -}}
{% endfor %}
</details>

{% endif %}
{% else %}
{{ decision.summary }}

{% if decision.artifact %}
{{ decision.artifact }}

{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if unit.reversible %}
**Reversible:**

{% for item in unit.reversible %}
<details>
<summary>{{ summary(item.title) }}</summary>

{{ item.text }}

</details>

{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endblock %}
{% block budgets_guidance %}
{#
  BUDGETS. A summary a person approving the plan reads in a minute, often on a phone: what
  the plan is sized for, what breaks first at 10x, which budgets it adds, which it changes
  and from what, how much longer every change's checks take because of it, and which
  existing budgets already cover the work. Every detail of a budget — its workload,
  evidence, command and budgets file, and for a changed one its full before and after —
  lives in the `## Budgets` section of the task that owns it, which each row and line links
  to, and every plan-level answer in full lives in the plan's own description. So this
  section carries none of that detail. Each link is the budget's `link` answer as it
  stands: a standard `<path>#<slug>` link to the owning task's `### Budget <id>` heading,
  relative to this document's file when the task is a local file, never a
  `#user-content-` fragment and never anchor markup; a tracker's own form is the plan
  copy's to write. A budget answer saved before `link` existed is linked at its
  `location`, with `#` and its `anchor` when it carries one, so a document saved then
  still renders its own body.

  The answers `plan_budgets` and `budgets` are exactly the plan's records: the project's
  `orchestrator.plan-budgets` metadata and every task's `orchestrator.budgets`, each budget
  with its task and where the store reports it, a version-3 changed budget's `previous`
  inside it. Copy them, and invent none. A plan with no added or changed task budget (no
  `file_change` exactly `add` or `change`) renders no Budgets section, even when it has
  unchanged budgets, effects, omissions, spike findings or a migration reason. Otherwise
  the section shows, in order, each part only when it has entries: Sizing; At 10×; the
  added budgets; the changed budgets whose `name`, `unit`, `direction` or `threshold`
  differs from `previous`, plus every changed budget with no `previous`, each showing
  previous → new for a moved name or target and saying when its previous value is not
  recorded; one collapsed block counting the version-3 changed budgets that differ in
  another field, one line each naming, without values, which of measure, inner measure
  reason, workload, command and measurement source (`source`) differ, or check runtime
  when that is its only other difference; the total check runtime the budgets add,
  followed by `check_runtime_summary`; the relevant unchanged (`file_change: none`)
  budgets, collapsed as existing coverage of the work; then in-scope Not budgeted and
  Spike findings. Every table has two columns, budget and target. The total sums each
  added version-3 budget's `check_runtime_seconds` and each changed version-3 budget's
  `check_runtime_seconds` minus its `previous` one, adds nothing for an unchanged budget,
  names a displayed budget with no version-3 figure as not estimated (showing no figure
  when none has one), and shows a negative total as a saving. Repo-wide effects never appear here. Filtering is presentation only:
  copy all three answers in full, including unchanged budgets and effects.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block budgets %}
{#- A budget's target as one cell, ≤ or ≥ before its threshold and unit. -#}
{% macro target(one) %}{% if one.direction == "min" %}≥{% else %}≤{% endif %}{{ one.threshold }} {{ cell(one.unit) }}{% endmacro %}
{#- Where a budget's name links: its owning task's budget heading. -#}
{% macro href(one) %}{% if one.link is defined %}{{ one.link }}{% else %}{{ one.location }}{% if one.anchor | default("") %}#{{ one.anchor }}{% endif %}{% endif %}{% endmacro %}
{#- Seconds as a person reads them: whole when whole, else to one decimal place. -#}
{% macro seconds(value) %}{% if value == (value | int) %}{{ value | int }}{% else %}{{ value | round(1) }}{% endif %}{% endmacro %}
{% set groups = namespace(added=[], rows=[], others=[], covering=[], unestimated=[], total=0, estimated=false) %}
{% for budget in budgets %}
{% set versioned = (budget.schema_version | default(0)) == 3 %}
{% if budget.file_change == "add" %}
{% set groups.added = groups.added + [budget] %}
{% if versioned %}
{% set groups.estimated = true %}
{% set groups.total = groups.total + budget.check_runtime_seconds %}
{% else %}
{% set groups.unestimated = groups.unestimated + [budget.name] %}
{% endif %}
{% elif budget.file_change == "change" %}
{% if versioned and budget.previous is defined %}
{% set was = budget.previous %}
{% set groups.estimated = true %}
{% set groups.total = groups.total + budget.check_runtime_seconds - was.check_runtime_seconds %}
{% if was.name != budget.name or was.unit != budget.unit or was.direction != budget.direction or was.threshold != budget.threshold %}
{% set groups.rows = groups.rows + [budget] %}
{% endif %}
{% set fields = namespace(names=[]) %}
{% for key, label in [["measure", "measure"], ["inner_measure_reason", "inner measure reason"], ["workload", "workload"], ["command", "command"], ["source", "measurement source"]] if was[key] != budget[key] %}
{% set fields.names = fields.names + [label] %}
{% endfor %}
{% if not fields.names and was.check_runtime_seconds != budget.check_runtime_seconds %}
{% set fields.names = ["check runtime"] %}
{% endif %}
{% if fields.names %}
{% set groups.others = groups.others + [{"budget": budget, "fields": fields.names}] %}
{% endif %}
{% else %}
{% set groups.rows = groups.rows + [budget] %}
{% set groups.unestimated = groups.unestimated + [budget.name] %}
{% endif %}
{% elif budget.file_change == "none" %}
{% set groups.covering = groups.covering + [budget] %}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% set omissions = namespace(items=[]) %}
{% for entry in (plan_budgets.checklist | default([])) if entry.not_applicable and (entry.in_scope | default(false)) == true %}
{% set omissions.items = omissions.items + [entry] %}
{% endfor %}
{% if groups.added or (budgets | selectattr("file_change", "equalto", "change") | list) %}
## Budgets

**Sizing.** {{ plan_budgets.sizing }}

**At 10×.** {{ plan_budgets.ten_x_summary }}

{% if groups.added %}
**Added budgets:**

| Budget | Target |
| --- | --- |
{% for budget in groups.added %}
| [{{ cell(budget.name) }}][{{ loop.index }}] | {{ target(budget) }} |
{% endfor %}

{% for budget in groups.added %}
[{{ loop.index }}]: {{ href(budget) }}{{ "\n" -}}
{% endfor %}

{% endif %}
{% if groups.rows %}
**Changed budgets:**

| Budget | Target |
| --- | --- |
{% for budget in groups.rows %}
{% set was = budget.previous if ((budget.schema_version | default(0)) == 3 and budget.previous is defined) else none %}
| [{% if was and was.name != budget.name %}{{ cell(was.name) }} → {% endif %}{{ cell(budget.name) }}][{{ groups.added | length + loop.index }}] | {% if not was %}{{ target(budget) }}, previous not recorded{% elif was.unit != budget.unit or was.direction != budget.direction or was.threshold != budget.threshold %}{{ target(was) }} → {{ target(budget) }}{% else %}{{ target(budget) }}{% endif %} |
{% endfor %}

{% for budget in groups.rows %}
[{{ groups.added | length + loop.index }}]: {{ href(budget) }}{{ "\n" -}}
{% endfor %}

{% endif %}
{% if groups.others %}
<details>
<summary>{{ groups.others | length }} {% if groups.others | length == 1 %}budget{% else %}budgets{% endif %} changed for other reasons</summary>

{% for other in groups.others %}
- [{{ other.budget.name }}]({{ href(other.budget) }}): {{ other.fields | join(", ") }}
{% endfor %}

</details>

{% endif %}
**Check runtime added:** {% if not groups.estimated %}not estimated for {{ groups.unestimated | join("; ") }}{% else %}{% if groups.total < 0 %}saves ≈{{ seconds(0 - groups.total) }} s{% else %}≈{{ seconds(groups.total) }} s{% endif %}{% if groups.unestimated %}, not estimated for {{ groups.unestimated | join("; ") }}{% endif %}{% endif %}.{% if plan_budgets.check_runtime_summary | default("") %} {{ plan_budgets.check_runtime_summary }}{% endif %}


{% if groups.covering %}
<details>
<summary>{{ groups.covering | length }} existing {% if groups.covering | length == 1 %}budget already covers{% else %}budgets already cover{% endif %} this work</summary>

| Budget | Target |
| --- | --- |
{% for budget in groups.covering %}
| [{{ cell(budget.name) }}]({{ href(budget) }}) | {{ target(budget) }} |
{% endfor %}

</details>

{% endif %}
{% if omissions.items %}
**Not budgeted:**

{% for entry in omissions.items %}
- {{ entry.concern }}: {{ entry.summary }}
{% endfor %}

{% endif %}
{% for entry in (plan_budgets.spike_findings | default([])) %}
{% if loop.first %}
**Spike findings:**

{% endif %}
- {{ entry.summary }}
{% if loop.last %}

{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
{% block accepted_opaque_guidance %}
{# Copy the export grants from the project's `onepipeline.export_edges` verbatim; never invent a pattern. #}
{% endblock %}
{% block accepted_opaque %}
